The file format is clear. save writes `{id}\n---\n{title}\n---\n{pinned}\n---\n{body
}` to `{dir}/{id}.note`. load_all reads all files in the directory.

Implementation of rename:
1. Find the note file for old_id: dir.join(format!("{}.note", old_id)).
2. Read it, parse with splitn(4, SEP) — verify len == 4 (otherwise error? If the note
 doesn't exist, return an error).
3. Construct the new note: new_id, new_title, same pinned flag, same body.
4. Write to the new file (save with the new note), then delete the old file.
5. Edge case: new_id == old_id → in that case save overwrites the same file and the
 delete would remove it. Handle this: if the filenames are the same, just write and
 don't delete.
6. What if the new file already exists? Overwrite it — that seems reasonable.
7. Error when the old note doesn't exist: return an io error (NotFound).

Also, note that load_all reads all files in the directory, including non-.note files
 — but that's not our concern.

Let's write the function:

```rust
pub fn rename(dir: &Path, old_id: &str, new_id: &str, new_title: &str) -> std::io
::Result<()> {
    let old_path = dir.join(format!("{}.note", old_id));
    let content = fs::read_to_string(&old_path)?;
    let parts: Vec<&str> = content.splitn(4, SEP).collect();
    if parts.len() != 4 {
        return Err(std::io::Error::new(
            std::io::ErrorKind::InvalidData,
            format!("note {} is not a valid note file", old_id),
        ));
    }
    let updated = Note {
        id: new_id.to_string(),
        title: new_title.to_string(),
        body: parts[3].to_string(),
        tags: crate::extract_tags(parts[3]),
        pinned: parts[2] == "1",
    };
    let same_file = old_id == new_id;
    save(dir, &updated)?;
    if !same_file {
        fs::remove_file(&old_path)?;
    }
    Ok(())
}
```

Note: save computes tags itself... actually save doesn't use tags — it just writes
 id, title, pinned, body. But Note has a tags field; set it appropriately.

Also, regarding atomicity: should we write to the new file and then delete the old
 one? Yes, this matches the requirements. If new_id == old_id, save overwrites and
 we don't delete.

